for i, seg in enumerate(segments):\\n            if alloc[i] >= seg[\\\"capacity\\\"]:\\n                continue\\n            priority = seg[\\\"importance\\\"] / (alloc[i] + 1)\\n            if priority > best_priority:\\n                best_priority = priority\\n                best_idx = i\\n            elif priority == best_priority and best_idx != -1 and i < best_idx:\\n                best_idx = i\\n        if best_idx == -1:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Cannot allocate remaining budget (capacity exhausted)\\\")\\n        alloc[best_idx] += 1\\n        remaining -= 1\\n    by_name = {}\\n    for i, seg in enumerate(segments):\\n        by_name[seg[\\\"name\\\"]] = alloc[i]\\n    total = sum(alloc)\\n    return {\\\"allocations\\\": alloc, \\\"by_name\\\": by_name, \\\"total\\\": total}\"}\n\nCheck: the code uses double quotes inside strings; we must escape them as backslash-doublequote. In the JSON string value, we need to escape backslashes and double quotes. For example, `case[\"segments\"]` becomes `case[\\\"segments\\\"]` in the Python string representation. But the JSON string itself must contain that sequence. So the JSON string will contain backslashes and quotes. We need to encode the Python source as a JSON string, meaning we need to escape any backslashes and double quotes in the source. The source includes double quotes, so we replace \" with \\\". Also backslashes if any (like newline \\n) are already represented as the two-character sequence backslash n. In JSON, newline should be represented as \\n (two characters). That's fine; we don't need to escape further. Actually in the source we have literal newlines in the Python code. But when we put the code as a JSON string value, we must replace actual newline characters with the two-character sequence \\n. So we need to convert the Python code into a single line with \\n for newlines. In my manual representation above I included actual newlines. For the JSON envelope, we need to put it as a