riority = -1.0\\n        for i, seg in enumerate(segments):\\n            if alloc[i] >= seg[\\\"capacity\\\"]:\\n                continue\\n            priority = seg[\\\"importance\\\"] / (alloc[i] + 1)\\n            if priority > best_priority:\\n                best_priority = priority\\n                best_idx = i\\n            elif priority == best_priority and best_idx != -1 and i < best_idx:\\n                best_idx = i\\n        if best_idx == -1:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Cannot allocate remaining budget (capacity exhausted)\\\")\\n        alloc[best_idx] += 1\\n        remaining -= 1\\n    by_name = {}\\n    for i, seg in enumerate(segments):\\n        by_name[seg[\\\"name\\\"]] = alloc[i]\\n    total = sum(alloc)\\n    return {\\\"allocations\\\": alloc, \\\"by_name\\\": by_name, \\\"total\\\": total}\n\nNow represent this as a JSON string: remove actual newlines and insert \\n, and escape double quotes. Also any backslash in the source (like for \\\" we already have backslash) we need to escape the backslash itself? Actually the source contains backslash only as part of the escape sequences for quotes inside strings. For example, `\\\"` in the source is a backslash followed by a double quote. In the JSON string, a backslash must be escaped as `\\\\`. So we need to replace each `\\` with `\\\\`. Also double quotes that are part of the source (the ones not used as string delimiters) must be escaped as `\\\"`. But careful: in the source, we have `\"` as part of string literals, like `case[\"segments\"]`. In the Python source, this is written as `case[\"segments\"]`. That contains a double quote character. When we embed that source as a JSON string, we need to replace that double quote with `\\\"`. Additionally, the backslash preceding it in the source is actually a backslash if written correctly? Wait, in my single-line representation above, I wrote `case[\\\"segments\\\"]`. That is a Python string representation: the backslash is an escape cha